  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 110 PM CDT Fri Oct 30 2026\n\nKDDC radar observations early Friday afternoon show a large \nprecipitation shield is moving slowly northward into western KS, \ncourtesy of a negatively-tilted upper level shortwave trough \nejecting northeastward from the southern plains as per recent water \nvapor satellite imagery. All HREF members and other short term \nguidance agree nearly our entire area will see light to moderate \nrainfall through the remainder of the day into the overnight period, \nbefore gradually tapering off from south to north. Precipitation \ntotals are expected to be in the 0.25-0.5\" range, with locally \nhigher amounts possible, along and south/east of a Liberal-Dodge \nCity-La Crosse line, while areas north/west of this line will see \nQPF gradually decrease from 0.25\" to just a trace to a couple \nhundredths near/along the KS/CO border.\n\nScattered rain showers may be ongoing early Saturday morning across \nour area as the left exit region of an approaching upper level jet \nstreak provides some synoptic forcing, but any/all precipitation \nshould erode by early afternoon. Temperatures daytime Saturday will \nbe near to below normal as overcast skies and the recent rainfall \nhelp to keep afternoon highs in the upper 50s to mid 60s.\n\nSunday through the end of the period, medium range ensembles suggest \na strong upper level wave will quickly move from the western CONUS \non Sunday onto the High Plains by late Monday/early Tuesday, \nfollowed by longwave ridging through the end of next business week.\nThis synoptic evolution will favor another chance for widespread\nprecipitation Monday and Tuesday as the aforementioned upper \ntrough passes through, succeeded by a dry period through Friday.\nIt should be noted that the probabilities of precipitation with\nthe early week trough may be overdone given the wave's open and\npositive tilt nature. These types of scenarios generally offer \nonly a limited opportunity for sufficient forcing for abundant \nprecipitation, and factoring in the possibility for convection \nover the southern plains to generate a potential vorticity lobe \nthat models struggle to capture and dragging the wave southward,\nthis event has true bust potential. That said, plenty of \nlingering moisture from today's precipitation helps to mitigate \nthese concerns somewhat, so trends will need to be monitored.",
